      en: "The Lower Sesan 2 Dam is the largest hydroelectric power facility in Cambodia, located in Stung Treng Province. It is situated at the confluence of the Sesan and Srepok rivers, two major tributaries of the Mekong River. The project was officially commissioned in late 2018 and has an installed capacity of 400 megawatts. It was developed as a joint venture between Cambodia's Royal Group and Chinese state-owned enterprises, including Huaneng Hydropower. The dam structure spans approximately 6.5 kilometers in length, making it one of the longest embankment dams in Southeast Asia. Its reservoir covers an area of roughly 30,000 hectares, providing substantial power to the national grid. The facility plays a pivotal role in Cambodia's strategy to achieve energy self-sufficiency and lower domestic electricity costs. The project's implementation required the relocation of several local communities and remains a landmark of modern industrial infrastructure in the country.",
